Gives each glyph in the glyph atlas a bin of its own. The atlas made a glyph's
bin id from the square root of its font stack's hash plus its code point, and
the shelf pack hands back the bin it already holds for an id. Two glyphs of
different font stacks whose ids matched shared one bin, so one drew the other.
In a 32-bit build such as WebAssembly, a font stack's hash is 32 bits, so the
square root stays under 65,536 and ids of Latin and CJK glyphs often match: a
label's "U" drew as "旧", and "市" as another glyph. Glyphs now take bins as
images do: an allocation keyed by the immutable glyph, which the font stacks
that hold the same glyph share.
Upstream: not proposed

